    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the yeast provides the bubbles but the bacteria does the flavor. does one usually dominate the other if the temperature changes? like if it gets really warm, does the bacteria take over and make it way too sour?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Generally, warmer temperatures favor the metabolic rate of the yeast, promoting faster proofing and lift. However, higher temperatures can also accelerate the activity of *Lactobacillus*, potentially leading to a quicker souring effect. If the kitchen gets very hot, the acid production from the bacteria can increase significantly, potentially giving you a much more pronounced and overwhelmingly sour flavor. Finding a moderate, consistent temperature helps maintain a good balance between lift and tang.",
      "source": "target",
